<component name="ProjectRunConfigurationManager">
  <!-- Launch the app under GoLand's debugger, so breakpoints in the bindings,
       the store and the domain files work.

       **The name matters.** GoLand derives the temporary binary's name from this
       configuration's name: "SunoMaker (Go debugger)" compiles to
       ___<N>SunoMaker__Go_debugger_.exe in its tmp directory. That is what makes
       "Kill stale debug processes" able to find this app's leftovers and only
       this app's — see #14, where this was called "App (Go debugger)", the same
       as Site Maker Pro's, and launching one killed the other's debug session.
       Rename this and that config's pattern together, or not at all.

       "Kill stale debug processes" runs first because a surviving instance of the
       previous run holds the output binary open and fails the build before any of
       this matters — see that config for why they survive.

       The two rebuild steps after it exist because only the Go half of this app
       is rebuilt by launching it. main.go embeds frontend/dist, so the UI in the
       window is whatever was last compiled to disk, not what is in the working
       tree — running with no rebuild silently debugs current Go source against a
       stale bundle. "Bindings: generate" must stay first: tsc checks the frontend
       against wailsjs/App.d.ts, so a newly added App method has to be regenerated
       before the frontend that calls it will compile.

       This is not a UI loop — it pays the rebuild on every launch and there is no
       Vite watcher behind it. For UI work, use "Wails: dev".

       The tags are not optional. Wails picks its CreateApp by build tag, and the
       one selected when none are given is a stub: it shows a message box reading
       "Wails applications will not build without the correct build tags" and
       returns a nil app, whereupon Run() returns nil and main() falls off the
       end — a green run that never started the app.

       - desktop, production: what `wails build` itself passes, and what makes
         CreateApp the real one rather than the stub.
       - debug: logs at LogLevel rather than LogLevelProduction, so the lines this
         app writes while starting up reach the Run panel.
       - devtools: right-click → inspect in the webview. `debug` does not imply it
         (IsDevtoolsEnabled is tag-gated on its own); the CLI passes both, so this
         does too.

       Deliberately no `-ldflags "-H windowsgui"`, which `wails build` does add on
       Windows: it detaches the console, and stdout is where the debugger's Run
       panel gets everything this app logs. -->
  <configuration default="false" name="SunoMaker (Go debugger)" type="GoApplicationRunConfiguration" factoryName="Go Application">
    <module name="suno-maker" />
    <working_directory value="$PROJECT_DIR$" />
    <go_parameters value="-tags desktop,production,debug,devtools" />
    <kind value="PACKAGE" />
    <package value="aimusicmaker" />
    <directory value="$PROJECT_DIR$" />
    <filePath value="$PROJECT_DIR$/main.go" />
    <method v="2">
      <option name="RunConfigurationTask" enabled="true" run_configuration_name="Kill stale debug processes" run_configuration_type="ShConfigurationType" />
      <option name="RunConfigurationTask" enabled="true" run_configuration_name="Bindings: generate" run_configuration_type="ShConfigurationType" />
      <option name="RunConfigurationTask" enabled="true" run_configuration_name="Frontend: build" run_configuration_type="js.build_tools.npm" />
    </method>
  </configuration>
</component>
